sometimes it feels like every other  movie and theatres and all superheroes  but how did we get here for the answer  we need to go all the way back to the  40s the first appearance of most modern  superheroes were serialized adventures  serials are so old that even five years  before Casablanca audiences were  blasting off with the first comic movie  Flash Gordon  in 1936 this was followed in short order  by serials for Captain Marvel Superman  Batman the shadow Zorro and the Phantom  but as serials were phased out of the  movie-going experience Studios seemed to  lose interest in costume crime-fighting  the wave of 60s and 70s superhero TV  shows didn't seem to translate well into  an interest in movies with the notable  exception of 1966 is Batman the movie  which was in fact filmed for television  and recut for theatrical release and  then nothing 12 years without comics  represented in film then in 1977 Star  Wars broke all previous box-office  records and changed the way Studios look  at movies executives went looking for  the next blockbuster in the 1940 serials  that had inspired George Lucas's vision  and what they found was Superman Richard  Donner's 1978 epic spawned plenty of  sequels and imitators both unfortunately  of rapidly diminishing quality  in 1989 Tim Burton's Batman redefined  what comic-book movies could be funny  exciting and darkly original Batman 89  showed Studios how to do a superhero  movie everybody needed to see much like  1978 Superman Batman created a wave of  sequels and interest in superhero  properties new and old but as with  Superman not all of these films were  well-received or even competently made  by the end of the decade pickings were  fairly slim 1996 is Batman and Robin had  killed the bat franchise and the  studio's had started to experiment with  relatively unknown properties like steel  spawn and blade in the 2000s the market  for comic movies exploded again driven  by movies like x-men and Sam Raimi's  spider-man action movies the standard  big movie experience for the last two  decades were replaced by films inspired  by comics the comic boom of the new  millennium started colorful then grew  darker inspired by the success of  Christopher Nolan's Batman films then  expanded its scope copying the extended  universe model created by marvel  all-in-all comic movies made since 2000  have grossed more than 20 billion  dollars in theaters internationally  which helps explain why most major  studios have the next 10 years of comic  film releases already planned what's  your favorite comic movie which comics  would you like to see in movie form  comment below and let us know how you  feel and be sure to click here to watch  our in-depth analysis of the history of  comic movies since 2000
 

